        "<strong>Pithom and Pi-Ramesses.</strong> Pi-Ramesses (modern Qantir) was the Nile-delta capital built under Ramesses II in the 13th century BCE; Pithom (Per-Atum) sat nearby. Their naming is one of the few Exodus details with direct archaeological tie-ins, and is a key data-point in debates over the date of the exodus (a 13th-century date fits these names; a 15th-century date would require the names to be later editorial updates).",
