        "Hebrew",
        "<strong>Mosheh (<em>מֹשֶׁה</em>) — Moses.</strong> The Hebrew etymology supplied by the text (<em>meshitihu</em>, “I drew him out”) is a folk-pun: grammatically the name is active (“one who draws out”), not passive (“drawn out”). Linguistically the name is almost certainly Egyptian — the same element seen in Thutmose, Ahmose, Ramesses (Egyptian <em>ms / mes</em>, “born of / son”). Pharaoh’s daughter would naturally have given an Egyptian name; the Hebrew text re-reads it as prophecy of his future role.",
        "User original",
