        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Exodus</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>Moses meeting Zipporah at the well in Midian, after defending her father&#x27;s flock from hostile shepherds, is read by Ephrem as the second well-betrothal after Jacob and Rachel: a prophet meeting his bride at a well of living water. The pattern recurs at Sychar, where the true Bridegroom meets the Samaritan woman at Jacob&#x27;s well (Jn 4). Tewahedo treats Ex 2:21 as a canonical link in the well-betrothal chain culminating in the Bridegroom-Christ.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Exodus II.10 (NPNF S2 V13, Gwynn/Schaff trans., Oxford 1898). PD.",
