        "<strong>K’vad-peh u-k’vad-lashon (<em>כְבַד־פֶּה</em> <em>וּכְבַד</em> <em>לָשׁוֹן</em>) — “heavy of mouth and heavy of tongue.”</strong> Two parallel idioms, neither a precise medical description. Ancient and medieval readers proposed everything from a stutter to a literal speech impediment to mere humility; the Hebrew is best taken as a self-deprecating idiom for being ill-suited to public speech. Aaron is given as Moses’ spokesman (4:14–16), and Moses’ own oratory in Deuteronomy seems to take place without difficulty.",
