        "",
        "I will harden his heart",
        "comm-rabbinic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Whose hand hardens Pharaoh’s heart?</strong> The book uses three distinct verbs (<em>chazaq</em>, <em>kashah</em>, <em>kaved</em>) and distributes the agency in a striking pattern: in chapters 7–9 Pharaoh hardens his own heart (or his heart simply “becomes hard”); only from chapter 10 onward does God explicitly do the hardening. Many readers (e.g. Origen, Maimonides, modern commentators like Childs) take the sequence to mean that judicial hardening confirms a will already fixed — God ratifies a choice Pharaoh has already made repeatedly.",
        "User paraphrase; references Origen, Maimonides; summarises Childs",
