        "<strong>Egyptian magicians.</strong> Egyptian wisdom literature actively cultivated reputations for snake-charming and staff-tricks; turning a staff into a serpent is attested in Pharaonic temple rites. The narrative grants the magicians initial parity (they replicate the first three signs) before the contest decisively breaks open. Later Jewish tradition (Targum Pseudo-Jonathan, 2 Tim 3:8) names two of them Jannes and Jambres.",
        "User paraphrase; references Targum, Pseudo-Jonathan",
