        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        "<strong>Etzba Elohim (<em>אֶצְבַּע</em> <em>אֱלֹהִים</em>) — “finger of God.”</strong> The Egyptian magicians’ confession is the first occurrence of an idiom that recurs at Sinai — the tablets of the Decalogue are written with the finger of God (Exod 31:18, Deut 9:10). The phrase implies a small, deliberate divine action — not the full “hand” of judgment but a measured sign. Jesus picks up the same idiom in Luke 11:20.",
        "User original",
