        "Hebrew",
        "<strong>Chamushim (<em>חֲמוּשִׁים</em>) — “armed,” “in fives,” or “marshalled.”</strong> The word is a four-way pun in Hebrew. Its root <em>chamesh</em> (“five”) can yield: armed (with five weapons or five soldiers abreast); in groups of fifty (a tactical fifth); only one fifth (i.e. the small remnant willing to leave); or simply organized in ranks. Translators must pick one; ancient versions chose differently (LXX <em>pemptē genea</em>, “in the fifth generation”).",
