        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        "<strong>Yam suf (<em>יַם־סוּף</em>) — “Sea of Reeds.”</strong> The Hebrew name does not say “Red Sea”; it says literally “Reed-sea.” The LXX rendered it <em>erythra thalassa</em> (“Red Sea”) — possibly because by the third century BCE the term had drifted to denote the larger Red Sea body, or possibly through a separate naming tradition. Geographically, candidate locations range from a marshy lake on the eastern Nile delta to the Gulf of Suez or Aqaba; the text’s precise location is debated, but “Reed-sea” fits a freshwater marsh better than the open salt sea.",
        "User paraphrase; references LXX",
