        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Exodus</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>Pharaoh&#x27;s host drowned in the very waters through which Israel walks dry is, for Ephrem, the canonical anti-type of baptism: what saves the believer destroys the pursuer. The devil and the powers of the old age are drowned in the font as Pharaoh was drowned in the sea. Tewahedo reads the renunciation of Satan in its baptismal rite as the liturgical correlate of the Red-Sea drowning.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Exodus XIV.10 (NPNF S2 V13, Gwynn/Schaff trans., Oxford 1898). PD.",
