        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        "<strong>Segullah (<em>סְגֻלָּה</em>) — “treasured possession.”</strong> The word denotes a private treasure of a king — items personally owned, distinct from the property of the realm. Akkadian and Ugaritic cognates confirm the royal-treasure sense. The term is used elsewhere of Israel only in Deut 7:6, 14:2, 26:18, and Mal 3:17 — always with the emphasis that the relation is personal and elective, not merely jurisdictional.",
        "User paraphrase; references Ugaritic",
