        "I am Yahweh",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The Decalogue’s preamble.</strong> The Ten Commandments begin not with a command but with a self-identification: the God who liberated. This places the whole moral framework on a relational and historical basis — obedience flows from covenant rescue, not abstract duty. Rabbinic tradition counts this verse as the first “word” (<em>dibbur</em>) of the ten; most Christian traditions count starting from “no other gods.”",
        "User original",
