    (
        33,
        20,
        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (363).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Sermo de Domino Nostro</em> <small>(c. 363 AD)</small><p>&#x27;There shall no man see me and live&#x27; — and yet Moses sees the Lord&#x27;s back as he passes by. Ephrem reads the verse as the canonical disclosure that full vision of God is reserved for the Incarnation: only when the Word is made flesh may humanity behold the divine face without being consumed. Tewahedo reads Ex 33:20 and 1 Jn 1:1 (&#x27;which we have seen with our eyes&#x27;) as a single canonical arc from veiled vision to face-to-face Word.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Sermo de Domino Nostro 7-8 (NPNF S2 V13). PD.",
    ),
    (
        33,
        20,
        "c",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Athanasius of Alexandria",
        "Athanasius of Alexandria (350).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Athanasius of Alexandria</strong> <em>Orationes contra Arianos III.14</em> <small>(c. 350 AD)</small><p>&#x27;And he said, Thou canst not see my face: for there shall no man see me, and live&#x27; — Athanasius marks ou dynēsē idein mou to prosōpon (thou canst not see my face) as the UNSEEABLE-FATHER / VISIBLE-WORD distinction. The Father-in-his-essence is unseeable (1 Tim 6:16 oude idein tis dynatai); what Moses is granted to see (LXX ta opisthia — &#x27;the back parts&#x27;) is the visible-condescension of the Word-pre-incarnate. Athanasius reads the Word as the agent of EVERY OT theophany (the burning bush, the pillar of cloud, the man-Yahweh at Mamre, the Sinai-glory) — the Father is never seen, only the Word who is the Father&#x27;s-form-and-image. This hermeneutic grounds the Tewahedo iconographic-convention: the Father is never directly-depicted, only the Word-incarnate.</p></aside>',
        "Athanasius of Alexandria, Orationes contra Arianos (Four Discourses Against the Arians, I-IV), in Select Writings and Letters of Athanasius, Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers (NPNF), Series 2, Vol. 4, ed. Archibald Robertson (Oxford / T&T Clark, 1892). PD. Greek text in Migne PG 26 (1857).",
    ),
