    (
        34,
        28,
        "",
        "forty days and forty nights",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Forty days, forty nights.</strong> The pattern recurs: Moses on Sinai (here, twice — 24:18 and 34:28), Elijah’s journey to Horeb (1 Kgs 19:8), Jesus’ wilderness fast (Matt 4:2). The number marks transitional periods of trial, preparation, or covenantal renewal. Jesus’ forty-day fast deliberately echoes both Moses and Elijah, the two figures who appear with him at the Transfiguration (Matt 17:3). The numerical pattern is theological grammar, not chronology.",
        "User original",
    ),
    # ---- heavy-pass batch 1: Exodus empties (chs 5, 9, 10, 11, 21, 23, 26, 27) ----
    (
        5,
        2,
        "",
        "Who is Yahweh",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Pharaoh’s question.</strong> Pharaoh’s "Who is Yahweh?" is the theological problem the rest of the book is going to answer. The Egyptian king’s question is asked from a polytheistic premise — he assumes Yahweh is one tribal deity among many — and the entire plague sequence is structured as an extended answer: Yahweh is the one whose power demonstrably surpasses every Egyptian deity (Hapi the Nile-god in plague 1, Heqet the frog-goddess in plague 2, etc.; cf. 12:12, "I will execute judgments against all the gods of Egypt"). The narrative is theologically self-conscious: the question Pharaoh asks ironically becomes the structuring rhetorical question of the next ten chapters. By 9:27 Pharaoh confesses "Yahweh is righteous"; by 12:31 he commands the exodus he initially refused. The narrative architecture is deliberately catechetical — the king who did not know Yahweh learns through plague, and the reader learns alongside him.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        5,
        21,
        "",
        "a  stench to be abhorred",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The Israelite foremen’s complaint.</strong> The Israelite foremen, beaten by Pharaoh’s slave-drivers because the people cannot meet the increased brick-quota, turn on Moses and Aaron with bitter accusation: their advocacy has made things worse. The episode is the first of many in which Israel itself becomes Moses’ opposition — a pattern that will run through the wilderness narratives and become the deuteronomistic theme of "stiff-necked" Israel. The verse is also psychologically realistic about how oppression frequently turns the oppressed against their advocates rather than the oppressors: when liberation produces immediate worsening before improvement, the advocates become the visible cause of the suffering. The narrative refuses to romanticize the people Moses is leading; it consistently shows them as ungrateful, frightened, and shortsighted. The honesty is theologically programmatic — Yahweh does not redeem Israel because Israel deserves redemption but because Yahweh has made covenant promises that bind even when the recipients are unworthy.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        9,
        6,
        "",
        "all the livestock of Egypt died, but of the livestock of the children of Israel, not one died",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The plague-distinction principle.</strong> Beginning here (in plague 5, the cattle-pestilence) and intensifying through plagues 6–10, the narrative consistently distinguishes Egyptian from Israelite suffering: only Egyptian livestock die, only Egypt experiences darkness (10:23), only Egyptian firstborn perish (12:29–30). The principle — Yahweh making "a distinction" (<em>palah</em>, 11:7) between Egyptian and Israelite — frames the exodus as judicial-discriminating action rather than indiscriminate plague. Theologically it answers the question why Israelites were not affected: not because they were geographically separate (Goshen is not far from Egyptian population centers) but because Yahweh’s judgment is targeted. The Passover ritual of ch 12 will operationalize the same principle at the family level: blood on the doorpost makes the household a discriminated zone. The exodus narrative’s entire theological logic — judgment against Egypt, deliverance for Israel — depends on this principle of discriminating divine action.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        9,
        16,
        "",
        "for this cause I have made you stand",
        "comm-catholic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>"For this purpose I have raised you up."</strong> The verse is one of the OT’s most theologically charged formulations of divine sovereignty over human history: Pharaoh’s very existence as Egyptian king is portrayed as instrumental to Yahweh’s revelation. Paul cites this verse explicitly at Rom 9:17 in his sustained meditation on divine election, sovereignty, and human responsibility — making it one of the foundational texts for the entire Christian theological tradition on predestination. Augustine, Calvin, and Aquinas all engage Rom 9 (and through it this verse) at length; Pelagius, Erasmus, and Arminius read it differently. The OT verse itself is deliberately vivid — Yahweh has not just permitted Pharaoh’s recalcitrance but has positioned the king "for this purpose." The same divine sovereignty over an opposing king’s heart will recur in Cyrus (Isa 45:1, "Yahweh’s anointed") and even Nebuchadnezzar (Jer 25:9, "my servant"). The verse encodes one of the OT’s most uncompromising formulations of the deity’s control over human political action.',
        "User paraphrase; references Augustine, Calvin, Aquinas",
    ),
    (
        10,
        21,
        "",
        "darkness over the land of Egypt, even darkness which may be felt",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The ninth plague.</strong> The three days of palpable darkness — Hebrew "darkness which may be felt" (<em>yamesh ḥoshekh</em>) — is the most cosmically pointed of the plagues: it directly attacks Ra, the Egyptian sun-god and supreme deity of the New Kingdom pantheon. The Egyptian theology of kingship rested on the Pharaoh’s identification with Ra’s son and the Pharaoh’s preservation of the cosmic order against the encroaching darkness. This plague reverses that order: darkness reigns over Egypt while the Israelites in Goshen have light (10:23). The plague functions as the immediate prelude to the climactic plague of the firstborn — Egyptian theology’s sun-god having been demonstrably overcome, the Pharaoh’s own status as Ra’s son will be the next target. The Wisdom of Solomon (17:2–18:4) develops the verse into an elaborate meditation on the spiritual darkness of Egypt’s injustice contrasted with the light of Israel’s deliverance. The plague is the theological hinge of the cycle.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        10,
        14,
        "",
        "no such locusts as they",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The locust plague.</strong> Locusts (Heb. <em>arbeh</em>) were the most feared agricultural disaster in the ANE — a single swarm could consume the food supply of a regional population. Egyptian sources confirm the destructive potential: the Famine Stela of the Ptolemaic era retrojects locust catastrophes onto Djoser’s reign as the worst-imaginable catastrophe; Ugaritic and Mesopotamian texts treat locust swarms as divine punishment by default. The plague’s theological significance: it strikes Egypt’s remaining agriculture after the hail of plague 7, depriving Pharaoh’s court of food — the chapter is the OT’s clearest articulation of plagues working in cumulative economic logic. Joel 1–2 takes the locust plague as the prophetic image of Yahweh’s eschatological "Day of the Lord," developing the Exodus pattern into apocalyptic vocabulary. The verse’s superlative formula ("never were there such locusts before, nor shall there be again") is part of the chapter’s deliberate intensification — each plague exceeds the last.',
        "User paraphrase; references Ugaritic",
    ),
    (
        11,
        5,
        "",
        "all the firstborn in the land of Egypt shall die",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The firstborn judgment.</strong> The threatened final plague targets the firstborn male of every Egyptian household — from Pharaoh’s royal heir to the firstborn of household servants, plus the firstborn of livestock. The targeting is theologically precise: "Israel is my firstborn son" (4:22), and Pharaoh’s refusal to release Yahweh’s firstborn is now answered by the death of Egypt’s firstborns. The principle — measure for measure (<em>middah k’neged middah</em> in later rabbinic vocabulary) — is one of the OT’s recurrent theological-judicial frameworks. The Passover ritual of ch 12 functions as the Israelite firstborn’s ransom: blood on the doorpost substitutes for the firstborn’s death, grounding the OT theology of substitutionary atonement that later Christian thought (especially Heb 9–10) develops Christologically. The verse is the structural pivot of the entire exodus narrative — the plague that Pharaoh cannot withstand, that produces the immediate release.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        11,
        7,
        "",
        "a dog won’t even bark or move its tongue",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The silence of the dogs.</strong> The detail — not a single dog will growl during the firstborn plague — registers the absolute supernatural quiet that will accompany the night of the angel of death’s passage through Egypt. The image is precise and chilling: in any normal night-event of mass death, dogs across the city would bark in chorus; the silence is the signature of divinely calibrated judgment. Egyptian dogs were associated with Anubis, the jackal-headed god of the dead — the silent dogs may be a small, pointed signal that Anubis himself has been overcome, that the Egyptian death-pantheon is paralyzed during the very night Yahweh visits Egypt with death. The detail also belongs to the chapter’s broader pattern of distinguishing Israel from Egypt: Israel’s livestock and people will be silent and unaffected, only Egypt will hear the cry of universal lamentation (12:30, "a great cry in Egypt; for there was not a house where there was not one dead"). The contrast of cosmic noise against absolute silence is the chapter’s most striking literary effect.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        21,
        24,
        "",
        "eye for eye, tooth for tooth",
        "comm-rabbinic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Lex talionis at Sinai.</strong> The first occurrence of the talion formula in the Pentateuch (also Lev 24:20, Deut 19:21). In its ANE legal context, the formula functions as a <em>limit</em> on retaliation: punishment must be proportional, never excessive. Hammurabi §§196–197 includes literal mutilations as the realized form; Hebrew rabbinic interpretation (Mekhilta on Exodus 21, b. Bava Kamma 83b–84a) understood the formula from the start as referring to monetary compensation calibrated to lost function rather than literal mutilation. Whether the rabbinic reading reflects original intent or interpretive softening is debated; literal mutilation is rare in actual Jewish legal practice. Jesus’ "you have heard it said… but I say to you" (Matt 5:38–39) does not abolish the proportionality limit but transcends it: refuse retaliation entirely. The verse is one of the most consistently misunderstood OT texts in popular reception — read as license for revenge when the original purpose was the opposite.',
        "User paraphrase; references Hammurabi, Mekhilta",
    ),
    (
        21,
        6,
        "",
        "his master shall bore his ear through with an awl",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The pierced-ear ceremony.</strong> The provision concerns the Hebrew slave whose six-year term has ended (cf. 21:2) but who chooses, out of love for his master’s family, to remain a permanent member of the household: he is brought to "God" (the household tribunal, or the local sanctuary) and his ear is pierced against the doorpost as a permanent mark. The ceremony combines several theological registers: the doorway as threshold of household identity (cf. 12:7, the Passover blood on doorposts), the ear as the organ of obedience (cf. Pss 40:6–8, "ears you have dug for me," cited Christologically at Heb 10:5–7), and the irreversible mark of permanent commitment. Paul’s self-designation as "slave of Christ Jesus" (Rom 1:1, Phil 1:1) draws on the same conceptual world — the freed person who chooses lifelong service. The Hebrew slave-law generally is one of the OT’s remarkable institutions: a worker is enslaved for at most six years, with the seventh year as automatic release (21:2) — far more humane than most ANE slave-codes.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        23,
        9,
        "",
        "You shall not oppress an alien",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The alien-protection clause.</strong> One of the OT’s most-repeated commands, occurring in slightly varying forms more than thirty times across the Pentateuch and prophets (cf. Lev 19:33–34, Deut 10:19, 24:14–22, Jer 7:6, Zech 7:10, Mal 3:5). The grounding rationale here is experiential: "you know the heart of an alien, since you were aliens in the land of Egypt." The provision uses Israel’s own historical trauma as the moral foundation for treatment of immigrants — Israel knows what it feels like to be a foreigner mistreated, and that knowledge becomes covenantal obligation. The principle is one of the OT’s clearest examples of empathy-based ethics: the experience of suffering is to be converted into the policy of refusing to inflict that same suffering on others. The contemporary political resonance is sharp; the provision has been a major reference point in modern Jewish and Christian advocacy for refugee and immigrant protection. Few OT verses have so direct an application across such a long stretch of legal-political history.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        23,
        19,
        "",
        "You shall not boil a young goat in its mother’s milk",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The first occurrence of the kid-in-milk prohibition.</strong> The verse appears three times in the Pentateuch (here, 34:26, Deut 14:21) — a triple attestation that, in rabbinic interpretation (b. Hullin 113b), generates the threefold separation of meat from milk in <em>kashrut</em>: not cooking together, not eating together, not benefiting from the mixture. The literal practice the verse forbids is documented in 14th-century-BCE Ugaritic ritual texts (KTU 1.23, the "Birth of the Gracious Gods"), where boiling a kid in milk appears as a Canaanite ritual gesture — making the verse a pointed prohibition of a specific known pagan rite. The rabbinic generalization is therefore an interpretive expansion well beyond the literal sense, but the literal sense itself is precisely calibrated: avoid the cultic practice of the surrounding peoples. The verse is one of the OT’s most striking examples of how a single ritual prohibition can generate an entire dietary tradition.',
        "User paraphrase; references Ugaritic",
    ),
    (
        26,
        33,
        "",
        "The veil shall separate the holy place from the most holy",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The veil and the gradient of holiness.</strong> The chapter institutes the architectural division that runs through Israelite cult: the courtyard (open to all Israelite worshippers), the holy place (priests only), and the most holy (high priest only, once per year on Yom Kippur). The veil between holy place and most holy is the most significant interior division in the entire tabernacle — and later the temple — encoding the principle that the deity’s presence is graded-access, not democratic. The Letter to the Hebrews (Heb 9:1–14, 10:19–22) takes the veil as the theological figure central to its argument: Christ’s death tears the veil (cf. Matt 27:51, the temple veil torn at Jesus’ death) and opens the most holy place to all who enter "by his blood." The veil and its tearing become one of the most theologically charged architectural images in the entire biblical canon. The verse here, instituting the veil, is the structural foundation for the entire later theology of access to divine presence.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        26,
        31,
        "",
        "a  veil of blue, and purple, and scarlet, and fine twined linen, with cherubim",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The cherubim on the veil.</strong> The veil is woven with figures of cherubim — guardian-creatures who flank the divine throne (cf. the cherubim atop the Ark, 25:18–22). The visual logic is precise: the veil itself participates in the throne-architecture, with the woven cherubim functioning as the throne’s outermost guardians and the ark’s sculpted cherubim as the innermost. The blue (<em>tekhelet</em>, the color of sky), purple (<em>argaman</em>, royal dye), and scarlet (<em>tola‘at shani</em>, costliest red) combine the most expensive ANE textile pigments — the veil is the most lavish single object in the tabernacle. The image-bearing veil carries theological weight given the second commandment’s prohibition on images (20:4): the chapter is comfortable with cherubim images in cultic context (cf. also the Solomonic temple’s carved walls, 1 Kgs 6:29), making clear that the prohibition is on images of deity, not images per se. The Israelite cult is image-rich at the level of guardian-figures while remaining strictly aniconic at the level of the deity itself.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        27,
        1,
        "",
        "the altar of acacia wood",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The bronze altar.</strong> The altar of burnt offering — five cubits square (about 7.5 feet on each side), three cubits tall, of acacia wood overlaid with bronze, with horns at the four corners — is the central object of the courtyard. Its prominence is structural: every offering Israel brings is consumed on this altar, and the entire economic life of the priesthood depends on what happens here. The altar’s horns become the asylum-points used for clinging (cf. 1 Kgs 1:50–53, Adonijah; 2:28–34, Joab; the institution of altar-asylum is implicit in Exod 21:14 and operationalized for the rest of the OT period). The "horns" themselves are one of the OT’s few archaeological points where physical reality has been recovered: the four-horned stone altar excavated at Tel Beersheba (eighth-century BCE) confirms the architectural feature precisely. The verse is the institutional foundation for Israelite sacrificial economy and one of the few tabernacle elements with continuous archaeological-textual correspondence.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        27,
        20,
        "",
        "pure olive oil beaten for the light",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The continual lamp.</strong> The chapter institutes the requirement that pure beaten olive oil be supplied for the seven-branched menorah, which is to burn continually in the holy place. The "continually" (Hebrew <em>tamid</em>) does not mean uninterrupted — the lamp is lit each evening and tended each morning (27:21, "from evening to morning") — but means perpetual as institution: every evening, without exception, the lamp is lit. The same vocabulary applies to the daily morning-and-evening burnt offering (29:38–42, the <em>tamid</em> offerings) — a continuous cultic rhythm that defines Israelite worship. The menorah’s continual flame becomes one of the most enduring symbols of Jewish identity through the post-temple period, with the synagogue’s "eternal lamp" (<em>ner tamid</em>) descending directly from this verse. Christian liturgical tradition appropriates the same vocabulary in the perpetual sanctuary lamp before the reserved sacrament. The verse is the OT root of one of the most durable continuous liturgical practices in religious history.',
        "User original",
    ),
    # ---- heavy-pass batch 2: Exodus empties (chs 29, 30, 35, 36, 37, 38, 39) ----
    (
        29,
        38,
        "",
        "two lambs a  year old day by day continually",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The tamid offering.</strong> The morning and evening lambs — one at dawn, one at twilight, every day without exception — constitute the <em>tamid</em> ("continual") offering, the spine of Israelite cultic time. Every priestly act, every individual offering, every festival sacrifice happens within the framework of these twice-daily lambs. The institution defined Jewish religious time for over a thousand years until the temple’s destruction in 70 CE, and the rabbinic synagogue tradition replaced the morning and afternoon <em>tamid</em> offerings with the morning and afternoon prayer services (Shacharit and Mincha) — the daily Jewish prayer cycle is the direct liturgical descendant of this verse. The Christian Liturgy of the Hours (Lauds and Vespers) preserves the same morning-and-evening structure. The verse encodes one of the OT’s most institutionally durable inventions: continuous liturgical time as the foundation of religious life.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        29,
        42,
        "",
        "a  continual burnt offering throughout your generations",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The meeting promise.</strong> The verse contains a remarkable theological claim: the daily burnt offering is the precondition for divine encounter — "where I will meet with you, to speak there to you." Yahweh’s availability for meeting Moses is structurally tied to the maintained <em>tamid</em> offering. The arrangement encodes one of the OT’s most theologically suggestive principles: divine presence is sustained through continuous liturgical maintenance, not through episodic crisis-response. The temple, by extension, becomes Yahweh’s appointed meeting-place precisely as long as the offerings continue; when they stop (as they did in the exile and in 70 CE), the meeting changes character. The Letter to the Hebrews (Heb 10:11–14) makes the contrast explicit: Christ’s "one sacrifice for sins forever" is read as superseding the daily-offering economy, removing the need for continuous sacrificial maintenance — a polemical-theological move that displaces the Levitical system. The verse is the foundation the NT writer is reframing.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        30,
        11,
        "",
        "Yahweh spoke to Moses, saying",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The half-shekel census tax.</strong> The verses immediately following (30:12–16) institute one of the OT’s most theologically loaded taxes: every Israelite male over twenty must pay half a shekel as "ransom for his life" when counted in a census, with the proceeds funding tabernacle service. The tax is deliberately egalitarian — "the rich shall not give more, and the poor shall not give less" (30:15) — a striking departure from typical ANE flat-tax-by-status systems. The half-shekel becomes one of the institutional foundations of Second Temple Jewish life: every Diaspora Jew sent the half-shekel annually to Jerusalem, and the Roman destruction of the temple in 70 CE redirected this revenue stream as the <em>fiscus Judaicus</em>, a punitive levy paid to Rome. Matthew 17:24–27 records Jesus paying the half-shekel temple tax for himself and Peter. The provision is one of the OT’s clearest articulations of cult-funding through universal small contribution rather than royal-or-priestly endowment.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        30,
        30,
        "",
        "sanctify them, that they may minister to me in the priest’s office",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The anointing oil.</strong> The chapter institutes a unique ritual blend — myrrh, sweet cinnamon, sweet calamus, cassia, and olive oil — used exclusively for anointing priests and tabernacle furniture (30:23–25, formula given). The oil is so holy that lay reproduction is a capital offense (30:33), and lay use is forbidden. The blend’s ritual exclusivity makes it one of the most carefully circumscribed objects in the cult; the rabbinic tradition (b. Horayot 12a) records the conviction that the original Mosaic anointing oil lasted into Solomon’s temple and was hidden during the Babylonian destruction. Anointing with this oil generates the term <em>mashiaḥ</em> ("anointed one") — applied to priests (here), kings (1 Sam 10:1, David, etc.), and ultimately, in NT Greek translation, to <em>christos</em>. The chapter’s detail is the linguistic-ritual root of the entire Christology that runs through Christian theology: "Christ" is not a name but a Levitical title.',
        "User original",
    ),
