    (
        35,
        1,
        "",
        "These are the words which Yahweh has commanded",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The construction sequence begins.</strong> Chapters 35–40 reverse the order of chapters 25–31: where the earlier chapters narrated Yahweh’s instructions to Moses, these chapters narrate Israel’s execution of those instructions. The doubling is one of the OT’s most extensive verbal repetitions — large stretches of vocabulary recur near-verbatim, with verbs shifted from imperative ("you shall make") to past indicative ("they made"). The function is theologically deliberate: covenantal obedience consists in the precise execution of divine instruction, and the narrative honors that execution by giving it equal textual space. The contrast with the golden calf episode (chs 32–34, narratively positioned <em>between</em> the instructions and the construction) is sharp: chapters 32–34 show what happens when Israel constructs without divine instruction; chapters 35–40 show what happens when Israel constructs in faithful response. The chapter division is the structural argument of the second half of the book.',
        "User original",
    ),
