    (
        38,
        8,
        "",
        "the mirrors of the ministering women",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The bronze mirrors.</strong> The basin where the priests washed before sanctuary service is made from the polished bronze mirrors donated by the women who served at the entrance of the Tent of Meeting. The detail is one of the OT’s most evocative — bronze mirrors were among the most personal possessions of Israelite women, used for daily grooming, likely the most expensive single item many of them owned. Their willingness to surrender them for cultic use (paralleled by the same generosity in the gold-jewelry donations of 35:22) is the chapter’s small portrait of communal sacrificial offering. The "ministering women" themselves remain mysterious — the same vocabulary in 1 Sam 2:22 attaches to women whom Eli’s sons abused at Shiloh, suggesting an institutional women’s role at sanctuary entrances throughout the period. The chapter preserves a glimpse of female cultic participation that other passages systematically minimize. Few OT verses encode so much theological texture in a single offering-detail.',
        "User original",
    ),
