    (
        39,
        1,
        "",
        "they made finely worked garments for ministering in the holy place",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The priestly vestments completed.</strong> The chapter executes the production of the high priest’s elaborate garments described in ch 28 — ephod, breastpiece, robe, tunic, turban, sash — combining blue, purple, scarlet, gold thread, twelve precious stones engraved with tribal names, and the Urim and Thummim oracular stones. The garments encode theological geography: every tribe’s name appears on the high priest’s breastpiece (39:14), so that when he enters the most holy place once a year, he carries the entire community symbolically into Yahweh’s presence. The principle — corporate representation through priestly garment — runs through later Christian sacramental theology (Heb 9:6–14, the high priestly garments as type for Christ’s incarnate humanity carrying redeemed humanity into the heavenly sanctuary). The detailed catalog of materials and procedures across chs 28 and 39 testifies to the centrality of vestment in Israelite cult: the priest is not just a person in a role but a person clothed in a role’s visible architecture.",
        "User original",
    ),
