        "Yahweh’s glory filled the tabernacle",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The glory-cloud arrives.</strong> The book’s closing scene: as soon as Moses finishes assembling the tabernacle, the cloud of Yahweh’s presence covers it and the divine glory (Hebrew <em>kavod</em>) fills the structure so densely that even Moses cannot enter. The image is of theological completion — the engineered meeting-place actually receives the deity it was designed to host. The same cloud-and-glory will later fill Solomon’s temple (1 Kgs 8:10–11) at the dedication, with the same effect (the priests cannot enter); will depart in Ezekiel’s vision (Ezek 10–11) at the temple’s defilement; and will return in Ezekiel’s eschatological temple (Ezek 43:1–5). The cloud thus traces the entire OT theology of divine presence with sanctuary across the canon. John 1:14 ("the Word became flesh and tabernacled [<em>eskēnōsen</em>] among us, and we beheld his glory") deliberately echoes this verse, claiming the incarnate Christ as the new tabernacle in which God’s glory dwells. The book of Exodus, which began with Israel groaning under Egyptian oppression (1:11), ends with Yahweh’s glory dwelling in their midst. The narrative arc is theologically complete.',
        "User original",
