        """<strong>’Ehyeh asher ’ehyeh (<em>אֶהְיֶה</em> <em>אֲשֶׁר</em> <em>אֶהְיֶה</em>).</strong> The most famous verbal puzzle in the Hebrew Bible. The verb <em>hayah</em> ("to be") is in the first-person imperfect, which can render as: "I am that I am," "I will be what I will be," "I am the one who is," "I cause to be what is." The LXX gives <em>egō eimi ho ōn</em> ("I am the One who is"), influencing all subsequent Greek-philosophical reading of God as Being itself. Maimonides took the Hebrew as denoting necessary existence; Ricoeur as fidelity-promise; Buber as presence ("I shall be present"). The form connects to the divine name YHWH (also from the root <em>h-y-h</em>).""",
