        "Note",
        """<strong>Tefillin.</strong> Together with Deut 6:8 and 11:18, this verse is the basis for the Jewish practice of binding scriptural texts in small leather cases on the arm and head. The literal interpretation became normative; the Karaites later argued for a metaphorical reading. The phrase "a memorial between your eyes" gives the head-tefillin its place; "a sign on your hand" gives the arm-tefillin its place. Pharisaic-rabbinic literalism here vs. spiritualizing readings represents one of the deepest interpretive divergences in Second Temple Judaism.""",
